UniverseConstraint.description:
A universe constraint is a control that restricts the set of securities
that can be traded. The constraint is defined by a pair of filter
expressions, one required and one optional. The two expressions are combined, such that
the `parameters` expression is applied to the subset of securities indicated by the `filter`
expression.

---

```json
{
  "filter": {
    "type": {
      "in": [ "treasury" ]
    }
  },
  "parameters": {
    "reg_s": {
      "equal_to": true
    }
  }
}
```

The above example constrains the set of tradeable treasury instruments to those
that are are Reg S eligible. Assuming no other controls are applicable, the affected
organizations' tradeable universe would be:

- Reg S-eligible treasuries;
- all munis;
- all corporates.

Universe constraints without a `filter` expression will be applied to all instruments. The
following control would restrict the tradeable universe to instruments denominated in USD:

```json
{
  "parameters": {
    "currency": {
      "equal_to": "USD"
    }
  }
}
```

Combined with the first control, the affected organizations' tradeable universe would be:

- Reg S-eligible treasuries denominated in USD;
- all munis denominated in USD;
- all corporates denominated in USD.

We could then add a third control:

```json
{
  "filter": {
    "type": {
      "in": [
        "corporate",
        "muninicpal",
      ]
    }
  },
  "parameters": {
    "and": [
      "reg_s": {
        "equal_to": true
      },
      "144a": {
        "equal_to": true
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

which would further restrict the tradeable universe to:

- Reg S-eligible treasuries denominated in USD;
- 144A-eligible munis denominated in USD that are not also Reg S-eligible; and
- 144A-eligible corporates denominated in USD that are not also Reg-S eligible.

### Field-to-field comparisons

Filter values can also reference other fields on the same instrument using the
`$field` syntax. For example, the following control ensures that an instrument's
`days_to_next_sink` is greater than its `call_notification_min`:

```json
{
  "parameters": {
    "days_to_next_sink": {
      "greater_than": { "$field": "call_notification_min" }
    }
  }
}
```

Field references are supported on all scalar comparison operators
(`equal_to`, `greater_than`, `less_than`, etc.) but not on array
operators (`in`, `not_in`).
